public class Solution {
    public TreeNode DeleteNode(TreeNode root, int key) {
        TreeNode parent = null;
        TreeNode current = root;

        while (current != null && current.val != key) {
            parent = current;
            current = key < current.val ? current.left : current.right;
        }

        if (current == null) {
            return root;
        }

        TreeNode replacement;

        if (current.left == null || current.right == null) {
            replacement = current.left ?? current.right;
        } else {
            TreeNode successorParent = current;
            TreeNode successor = current.right;

            while (successor.left != null) {
                successorParent = successor;
                successor = successor.left;
            }

            if (successorParent != current) {
                successorParent.left = successor.right;
                successor.right = current.right;
            }

            successor.left = current.left;
            replacement = successor;
        }

        if (parent == null) {
            return replacement;
        }

        if (parent.left == current) {
            parent.left = replacement;
        } else {
            parent.right = replacement;
        }

        return root;
    }
}
```

The root may change, so the method returns the replacement when the deleted node is the root. A missing key does not modify any links. This method also changes the input tree in place.

Time complexity: O(h).
Space complexity: O(1).

The predecessor is the largest node in the left subtree. It fits in the deleted node's position because every value on the left is smaller, while the right subtree is still larger. The recursive return value reconnects the changed subtree to its parent.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) : data(val), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
public:
    TreeNode* deleteNode(TreeNode* root, int key) {
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        if (key < root->data) {
            root->left = deleteNode(root->left, key);
        } else if (key > root->data) {
            root->right = deleteNode(root->right, key);
        } else {
            if (root->left == nullptr) {
                return root->right;
            }
            if (root->right == nullptr) {
                return root->left;
            }

            TreeNode* predecessor = root->left;
            while (predecessor->right != nullptr) {
                predecessor = predecessor->right;
            }

            root->data = predecessor->data;
            root->left = deleteNode(root->left, predecessor->data);
        }

        return root;
    }
};
```

A leaf returns null, and a node with one child returns that child. For instance, after deleting 7 from a tree rooted at 5, the call reaches the right subtree and simply reconnects the remaining child or null.

If the tree is very unbalanced, the recursion can become as deep as the number of nodes.

Time complexity: O(h).
Space complexity: O(h) for the call stack.

The search is iterative, but the actual removal uses a small splice helper. When both children exist, the whole left subtree becomes the replacement and the old right subtree is connected to the rightmost node of that left subtree.

```go
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * type TreeNode struct {
 *     Val int
 *     Left *TreeNode
 *     Right *TreeNode
 * }
 */

func deleteNode(root *TreeNode, key int) *TreeNode {
	parent := (*TreeNode)(nil)
	current := root

	for current != nil && current.Val != key {
		parent = current
		if key < current.Val {
			current = current.Left
		} else {
			current = current.Right
		}
	}

	if current == nil {
		return root
	}

	replacement := splice(current)
	if parent == nil {
		return replacement
	}

	if parent.Left == current {
		parent.Left = replacement
	} else {
		parent.Right = replacement
	}
	return root
}

func splice(node *TreeNode) *TreeNode {
	if node.Left == nil {
		return node.Right
	}
	if node.Right == nil {
		return node.Left
	}

	rightmost := node.Left
	for rightmost.Right != nil {
		rightmost = rightmost.Right
	}
	rightmost.Right = node.Right
	return node.Left
}
```

For a target with two children, the left subtree stays on the left side. Attaching the old right subtree at its largest node keeps every value in the correct order. For a tree containing only one node, deleting that key returns nil.

Time complexity: O(h), where h is the height.
Space complexity: O(1).

This version keeps the parent while searching for the key. After finding the node, a helper returns the subtree that should take its place. For two children, the right subtree is attached to the largest node in the left subtree, so no value copying is needed.

