```javascript
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * class TreeNode {
 *      constructor(val = 0, left = null, right = null){
 *          this.data = val;
 *          this.left = null;
 *          this.right = null;
 *      }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    deleteNode(root, key) {
        const dummyParent = new TreeNode(-1);
        dummyParent.left = root;
        let parent = dummyParent;
        let curr = root;

        while(curr) {
            if(curr.data === key) {
                let leftNode = curr.left;
                let rightNode = curr.right;
                if(rightNode) {
                    let temp = rightNode;
                    while(temp.left !== null) {
                        temp = temp.left;
                    }
                    temp.left = leftNode;
                    if(parent.left === curr) {
                        parent.left = rightNode;
                    } else {
                        parent.right = rightNode;
                    }
                } else {
                    if(parent.left === curr) {
                        parent.left = leftNode;
                    } else {
                        parent.right = leftNode;
                    }
                }
                curr.left = null;
                curr.right = null;
                break;

            } else if(curr.data < key) {
                parent = curr;
                curr = curr.right;
            } else {
                parent = curr;
                curr = curr.left;
            }
        }

        if(parent === dummyParent) {
            return dummyParent.left;
        } else {
            return root;
        }
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
$O(H)$, where $H$ is the height of the tree. In the worst-case scenario (a skewed tree), this degrades to $O(N)$. For a balanced BST, it operates in $O(\log N)$ time. Both searching for the key and finding the leftmost node in the right subtree are bounded by the tree's height.

**Space Complexity:**
$O(1)$ auxiliary space. The traversal and structural updates are done iteratively using only a few pointers (`dummyParent`, `parent`, `curr`, `temp`, etc.), meaning no recursive call stack or additional data structures are required.

